To change the background color of an Adaptive Card in the Bot Framework, you can use the `backgroundColor` property in the card's JSON definition. Here's an example:

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "version": "1.0",
  "backgroundColor": "lightblue",
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "TextBlock",
      "text": "Hello, world!",
      "size": "large"
    }
  ]
}
```

In this example, the background color of the entire Adaptive Card is set to "lightblue". You can replace "lightblue" with any valid CSS color value, such as a color name, hex code, or RGB value.

Note that not all channels may support the `backgroundColor` property, so it's a good idea to test your card on the target channel(s) to ensure it works as expected.

If you're using the Bot Framework SDK, you can create an Adaptive Card by constructing the JSON object in your code. For example, in C#:

```csharp
var card = new AdaptiveCard("1.0")
{
    BackgroundColor = "lightblue",
    Body = new List<AdaptiveElement>
    {
        new TextBlock
        {
            Text = "Hello, world!",
            Size = TextSize.Large
        }
    }
};

var activity = MessageFactory.Attachment(card.ToAttachment());
await turnContext.SendActivityAsync(activity);
```

In this example, the `BackgroundColor` property is set to "lightblue" using the Adaptive Card SDK for C#. The card is then sent as an attachment in a message.

Remember to include the necessary namespaces and install the required packages for the Adaptive Card SDK in your project.

